 #edb/ref 17592186262632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186405043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563824 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563828 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563837 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563840 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563841 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563844 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563860 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186563859 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1045 "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "eb431efc-453b-5866-adc7-559d3c697e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20585d4c-063f-50c6-8591-a785c62137ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176313 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1061 33843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efebb741-9ea8-56f3-9f79-7c340d1fe301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1061 35775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "736cedc4-b5c1-5e97-b975-c6db4fd154c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1061 35776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b3de726-ead0-5369-a1fe-3dcc61b38bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1061 35777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f69ccccf-5d34-5f08-95d0-7af5d0c7628b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1065 5838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b5a23c7-4ccf-5122-8b5f-98fbabb0e367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257271 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257393 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45df6382-7cbc-520f-a94b-e0654045f8da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257928 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "80f5da35-1f8d-5b8e-98bc-a87edfbce6e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258683 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9166bd6-4167-5918-9672-1af0c9421726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186258810 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^